7. Code review risks and recommendations
•	Keep configuration claims precise: scanOnly is task-driven, while daysBefore and trainingMode were hardcoded in the last reviewed source.
•	Validate multi-value requiredCourse parsing, whitespace, duplicate training records, and duplicate notifications for a user/course.
•	Check behavior when metadata resolution returns null, Bundle rather than ManagedAttribute, or an unmatched catalog record.
•	Separate counters for scanned users, qualifying users, submitted emails and confirmed delivery; allUsersNotified previously included users who received no email.
•	Validate date semantics (SQL Server calendar-day boundaries, time zones, and exact 7/15/30 matching).
•	Review manager CC and global redirect behavior before any unrestricted non-scan-only execution.
•	Retain isolation between warning notification and existing revocation enforcement.
•	Investigate the SAP ECC test-role warning separately; do not claim target provisioning succeeded.
8. Remaining tests and deployment gates
•	Negative test: uncheck revokeOnExpTraining for a controlled entitlement, rerun scan-only, and confirm exclusion; restore metadata afterward.
•	End-to-end email test: verify intended recipient, manager CC suppression/redirect, SMTP handoff and actual mailbox receipt.
•	Regression tests: multiple users/courses, multiple entitlements, missing identities, no catalog match, missing manager/email, and duplicate training records.
•	Review task configuration and safety flags before promoting changes beyond TEST.
9. Review meeting talk track
We enhanced the existing IIQ NERC notification rule to discover expiring training courses from the LMS database and identify users through the existing table library. We reused the policy library to identify training-dependent access, then added a stricter entitlement-level filter requiring a matching requiredCourse and revokeOnExpTraining=true. We also introduced task-controlled scan-only execution to validate eligibility without sending emails. To support testing, we built read-only inventory, expiration, and entitlement-mapping diagnostics. The positive TEST scan increased the qualifying access count from zero to one after assigning a controlled entitlement, with zero emails sent as expected. A later non-scan-only run submitted one warning email, but receipt has not been verified. Negative checkbox testing, delivery verification, and regression checks remain before deployment.
10. Likely reviewer questions
Why separate dynamic rule? To isolate enhancement testing and compare with original behavior before deployment.
Why use both libraries? Table library finds training users; policy library evaluates training dependencies of assigned access.
Why add another filter? Existing NERC/HIPAA mappings can mark access as training-dependent without meeting the explicit JIRA checkbox requirement.
Does it revoke access? No; it sends advance warnings. Existing enforcement handles revocation.
What proved positive behavior? Qualifying access count changed from 0 to 1 in scan-only TEST after configuring the matching entitlement.
Why did counts drop later? Exact calendar-day filters are date-sensitive; verify database today and DaysLeft to confirm.
Was email delivered? One send was submitted successfully, but mailbox delivery was not confirmed.
What remains? Negative testing, recipient/SMTP delivery, edge cases, and regression review.
